-- title:
--   A coded configuration lies in the observer exactly when the machine has halted
-- statement:
--   The recorder is a three-stack machine that simulates a Turing machine $M$ reversibly. A configuration has a control state and three infinite stacks: left, right and history. Stack letters are work symbols $a$, marked symbols $\hat a$, a boundary marker, and records of executed transitions. Each step replaces finite prefixes of the three stacks and the control according to one row of a finite table. A configuration is coded by the point $o+(c(s_0),c(s_1),c(s_2))$. Here $c$ is the base-$B$ stream code of a stack, using odd digits, and $o=(4k,0,0)$, with $k=0$ for the halting ready state and $k\ge1$ for every other control state. Let the observer be the cube $(-1,2)^3$. Then
--   $$
--   \mathrm{code}(C)\in(-1,2)^3\iff\text{the control of }C\text{ is the halting ready state}.
--   $$
--
--   This is how halting becomes a geometric event: the particle enters the observer.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/OAI/MathematicalPhysics/NavierStokes/BalancedTransport/RecorderSimulation.lean#L117-L138

